WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:10.530
so by early December 1914 the punishing
advance of the Germans have been stopped

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:19.529
by the Allies before the Germans made it
to Paris but had been stopped at a great

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:31.439
cost a sickening cost but now the armies
had settled into the trenches and we

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:42.660
know what the trenches were hell not
some metaphorical approximation of hell

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:53.570
but actual hell hell on earth artillery
mortars machine gun fire snipers

00:00:53.570 --> 00:01:02.790
shrapnel screams of the wounded and
dying and on top of that the environment

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:14.930
rain and sleet and snow and flooded
trenches and freezing cold and of course

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:33.330
death was everywhere everywhere and
there seemed no end to the horror but on

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:46.200
Christmas Day 1914 the horror did stop
if only for a short time while men for a

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:59.490
moment recognized the men they were
fighting against as other men as other

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:11.039
men with the same hopes and dreams and
desires that they had themselves and the

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:20.370
chief
of all hope where's peace and this is an

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:29.090
excerpt of how the Christmas truce
unfolded according to Henry Williamson a

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:42.629
British Army officer who is there and
here's what he wrote four weeks we had

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:51.180
lived in flooded trenches the Germans
were 80 yards away our trench was and

00:02:51.180 --> 00:03:00.239
fellated we lost many men shot by
snipers night after night since the

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:05.639
tailing off the Battle of Ypres x' we
had toiled in the parapets filling

00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:11.129
sandbag with clay mud squelched through
muddy lagoons of woodland tracks

00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:20.879
carrying rations duckboards pumps and
ammunition we were volunteers rushed out

00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:29.480
to help general French's shattered
Expeditionary Force a few weeks before

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:38.910
we had been schoolboys bank clerks
undergraduates medical students now our

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:45.989
lives were ravaged some of us the young
ones who thought of their mothers were

00:03:45.989 --> 00:04:01.200
near to despair we were without hope
without horizon at first

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:07.290
trench life had been interesting even
enjoyable it was fun cooking our own

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:14.160
bacon and making tea in the wood while
shrapnel cracked overhead good sport

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.079
stalking the wild geese in the marshes
satisfying to feel the soft hairs of our

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:31.590
unshaven chins the regulars were decent
chaps heroes of Mons but the rains fell

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:40.830
and the trenches filled almost waist
high after a few days we could scarcely

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:47.550
move our legs nor did we seem to need
food at night we dragged ourselves out

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:52.490
of the ditches and moved about uncaring
of bullets aimed at random in the dark

00:04:52.490 --> 00:05:00.030
all night we worked carrying parties
pumping fatigues parapet building at

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:09.020
dawn we slid in the water again and set
ourselves to endure the gray daylight

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:16.890
even now so long afterwards when I hear
rain on the tiles overhead the ghost of

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:25.710
that time makes me draw the blankets
closer around my neck on Christmas Eve

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:32.070
of 1914 we were in the support line
about 200 yards inside the plug stared

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:41.610
wood it was freezing our overcoats were
as stiff as boards our boots were too

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:49.650
hard to move but we rejoiced as the mud
was hard too also happy thought we would

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:57.240
be able to sleep that night inside a new
block house of oak boughs and sandbags

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:06.050
called Piccadilly Hotel no bed but the
cold earth no blankets even but sleep

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:09.050
sleep

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:16.840
then came a message from brigade
headquarters broad I think by second

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:24.250
lieutenant Bruce Barron's father of the
Warrick's wiring parties were required

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:33.070
in no-man's land all night and there
would be a moon we would have to work

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:37.000
only 50 yards from the German machine
guns in the white house opposite the

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:45.490
eastern edge of the wood two hours later
we filed out of the dark trees into the

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:52.000
naked moonlit terror of no-man's land
holding our shovels besides our faces in

00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:01.240
hope of protection against the expected
mortar blast the moon was high and white

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:08.710
among the frozen clouds we were visible
someone slipped with a Clank of a spade

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:16.330
or rifle we flung ourselves on our faces
and waited but the battlefield was as

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:25.659
silent as the moon for an hour we worked
in silence in a most mysterious sound

00:07:25.659 --> 00:07:31.960
lessness what had happened we began to
talk naturally as we drove in stakes and

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:38.650
pulled out concertinas of prepared wire
there was no rifle fire either up or

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:47.320
down the line from way up north beyond
appraise to south beyond our mentiras

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:57.580
and the French army at midnight we heard
laughing as we worked we heard singing

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:05.050
from the German lines carols the tunes
of which we knew I noticed a very bright

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:11.919
light on a tall pole raised in their
lines down opposite the east lanx trench

00:08:11.919 --> 00:08:20.020
in front of the convent a Christmas tree
with lighted candles was set on their

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:22.529
parapet

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:30.529
the unreal moonlight life went on
happily cries of c'mon over Tommy we

00:08:30.529 --> 00:08:38.199
won't fire at you a dark figure
approached me hesitatingly a trap I

00:08:38.199 --> 00:08:43.010
walked towards it with a bumping heart
Merry Christmas

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:52.550
English friend we shook hands
tremulously then I saw that the light on

00:08:52.550 --> 00:09:00.290
the pole was the morning star the star
in the east it was Christmas morning all

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:07.160
Christmas Day gray and khaki figures
mingled and talked in no-man's land the

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:14.630
picks and spades rang in the hard ground
it was strange to stare at the dead we

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:22.360
had only glimpsed at swiftly from the
trenches the shallowest graves were dug

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:28.430
filled and set with crosses knocked
together from lengths of rationed

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:37.579
boxwood marked with indelible pencil for
king and country for fatherland own free

00:09:37.579 --> 00:09:50.329
height fatherland and freedom freedom
freedom how was this we were fighting

00:09:50.329 --> 00:09:57.310
for freedom and our cause was just we
were defending Belgium civilization

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:06.949
these fellows in gray were good fellows
they were strangely just men like

00:10:06.949 --> 00:10:14.769
ourselves how can we lose the war
English comrade our cause is just

00:10:14.769 --> 00:10:20.269
we are ringed with enemies the war was
thrust on us we are defending our

00:10:20.269 --> 00:10:30.529
parents our homes our German soil the
most shaking staggering thought that

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:34.070
both sides thought they were fighting
for the same cause

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:44.540
the war was a terrible mistake people at
home did not know this then the idea

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:51.139
came to the young and callow soldier
that if only he could tell them at home

00:10:51.139 --> 00:10:56.569
what was really happening and if the
German soldiers told their people the

00:10:56.569 --> 00:11:07.130
truth about us the war would be over but
he hardly dared to think it even to

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:17.360
himself the next day was quiet and the
next waving hands from the trenches by

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:26.329
day singing and reflected blaze of
trench bonfires at night it was a lovely

00:11:26.329 --> 00:11:35.180
time on the third afternoon came a
message from the Germans at midnight our

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:41.870
staff officers visit and we must fire
our automatic pistol on but we will fire

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:50.569
higher nevertheless please keep
undercover at 11 p.m. Berlin midnight we

00:11:50.569 --> 00:11:55.300
saw flashes going away into the air

00:11:56.230 --> 00:12:04.160
two days later an army order came from
GHQ to the effect that men found

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.510
fraternizing with the enemy would be
court-martialed and if found guilty

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:18.019
would suffer the death penalty and again
in that place the very lights soared

00:12:18.019 --> 00:12:26.170
over no man's land at night then bullets
cut showers of splinters from trees and

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:43.449
sometimes human flesh and bone so hope
sank into the mud again but did not die

00:12:43.449 --> 00:12:50.720
despite a withering anew as each
or human unit fell in machine gun and

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:56.600
mortar blast and colossal reverberating
rending of the shells of those four

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:12.410
years the years whose truth seems to be
in communicable sometimes as one listens

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:22.630
to what people say here in the England a
generation died for it seems like almost

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:38.230
pre-war again can it be that we lack
imagination to see the other fellow as

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:41.230
ourselves

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:59.480
Henry Williamson continued to fight
he was eventually wounded by gas then

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:05.360
when he was declared unfit to serve in
the army he then volunteered for the Air

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:12.740
Force to see if that could get him back
to the front but he didn't make it back

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:27.019
to the front luckily because the war
ended the war ended but not before 20

00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:49.959
million were wounded and 17 million men
were killed men just normal men gone

00:14:54.880 --> 00:15:10.750
so this holiday season please think of
those men and women around the world in

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:24.819
the trenches now risking life and limb
for what they believe in for freedom and

00:15:24.819 --> 00:15:34.089
think of the civilians trapped in those
hellish places suffering in the

00:15:34.089 --> 00:15:50.529
merciless paths of war and finally as
Henry Williamson asked let us not lack

00:15:50.529 --> 00:16:09.420
the imagination to think to think of
other men as men men like ourselves men

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:12.750
like us

00:16:17.089 --> 00:16:19.149
you

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:28.110
Merry Christmas to all and may there be
peace on earth

